 Show description for 2c80fe1\nL305: \nL306: 【71†quangvdao】\nL307: \nL308: committed\nL309: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPower circuits: a new arithmetization for GKR-styled sumcheck (https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1611)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1611\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 71\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2023/1611\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Power circuits: a new arithmetization for GKR-styled sumcheck】\nL32: \nL33: Lev Soukhanov, Ethereum Foundation\nL34: ##### Abstract\nL35: Goldwasser-Kalai-Rothblum protocol (GKR) for layered circuits is a sumcheck-based argument of knowledge for layered circuits, running in $\\sim 2\\mu \\ell$ amount of rounds, where $\\ell$ is the amount of layers and $\\mu$ is the average layer logsize.\nL36: For a layer $i$ of size $2^{\\mu_i}$ the main work consists of running a sumcheck protocol of the form \\[\\underset{x,y}{\\sum} \\text{Add}_i(x,y,z)(f(x)+f(y)) + \\text{Mul}_i(x,y,z)f(x)f(y)\\] over a $2^{2\\mu_i}$-dimensional cube, where $\\text{Add}_i(x,y,z)$ and $\\text{Mul}_i(x,y,z)$ are (typically relatively sparse) polynomials called \"wiring predicates\". We present a different approach, based on the (trivial) observation that multiplication can be expressed through linear operations and squaring.\nL37: This leads to the different wiring, which is marginally more efficient even in a worst-case scenario, and decreases the amount of communication $\\sim 2 \\times$ in the case where wiring predicates are sparse.\nL38: ##### Metadata\nL39: \nL40: Available format(s)\nL41:     【17†PDF】\nL42: Category\nL43:     【18†Foundations】\nL44: Publication info\nL45:     Preprint.\nL46: Keywords\nL47:     【19†verifiable computation】【20†circuit evaluation】【21†GKR】\nL48: Contact author(s)\nL49:     0xdeadfae @ gmail com\nL50: History\nL51:     2023-10-20: approved\nL52:     2023-10-17: received\nL53:     【22†See all versions】\nL54: Short URL\nL55:     【23†https://ia.cr/2023/1611†ia.cr】\nL56: License\nL57: \nL58: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL59: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL60: \nL61:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2023/1611,\nL62:           author = {Lev Soukhanov},\nL63:           title = {Power circuits: a new arithmetization for {GKR}-styled sumcheck},\nL64:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2023/1611},\nL65:           year = {2023},\nL66:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1611}\nL67:     }\nL68: \nL69: \nL70: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · LayerZero-Labs/akita · GitHub (https://github.com/LayerZero-Labs/akita/releases)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/LayerZero-Labs/akita/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3:   * Products\nL4: \nL5:     *       * 【1†GitHubRepository hosting, code review, and collaboration】\nL6:       * 【2†CopilotAI coding age